{"author":"Rebar Bot Nava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","data":{"body":"Investigation so far (2026-10-30, main 38f8587f65d, worktree .rebar/worktrees/fix-terminal-retire, evidence /Users/joeoakhart/rebar/.rebar/worktrees/fix-terminal-retire/.rebar/scratch/hammer-17068): (1) The -n 4 combination reproduces: 46 FAILED lines, all on worker gw3 (gw0-2: 0), first at test_at_most_one_invariant.py::test_reconcile_once_invokes_invariant_after_fetch, immediately after gw3 ran tests/interfaces/facades/test_title_validation.py. Per-worker order: /Users/joeoakhart/rebar/.rebar/worktrees/fix-terminal-retire/.rebar/scratch/hammer-17068/gw3.txt; gw3's 45 preceding files: /Users/joeoakhart/rebar/.rebar/worktrees/fix-terminal-retire/.rebar/scratch/hammer-17068/gw3-files.txt. (2) Each of those 45 files alone followed by test_reconcile_once.py (-n 0, -p no:randomly): all pass. (3) All 45 in gw3 order then test_reconcile_once.py serially (-n 0): 697 passed. So the failure needs concurrency: state shared ACROSS workers (filesystem: the worktree's rebar.toml/.rebar/.tickets-tracker, HOME, a shared cache file) rather than in-process leakage. (4) Mechanism of the failure: tests/unit/reconciler/conftest.py autouse pins JIRA_PROJECT=DIG; runtime._runtime_from_snapshot reads jira.project from _operation_config.compose_operation_snapshot(...).values, which came back empty, so assert_cloud_scope_ready raised. Next: find what another worker writes that makes config resolution drop the env JIRA_PROJECT (e.g. a [jira] project = '' written to a shared rebar.toml, or a config/env file read with higher precedence)."},"env_id":"492a5c71-6cda-48a6-8134-02ff6a672739","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1793396473472311001,"uuid":"aa985817-d785-4950-a690-4094e99340d7"}